## Google Vertex AI

Supports streaming through `sendMessage` and structured JSON output through `generateStructured` ([validated by: streams text chunks from Vertex AI response](../../src/providers/vertex/vertexProvider.test.ts#L69), [validated by: returns parsed JSON from Vertex response](../../src/providers/vertex/vertexProvider.test.ts#L489)).

<!-- doc-block: none -- a composed provider configuration; its fields are checked through src/config.ts by typecheck -->
```typescript
const engine = createHalEngine({
  provider: {
    type: 'vertex',
    projectId: 'my-gcp-project',
    location: 'europe-west4',
    modelId: 'gemini-2.5-pro',
    maxTokens: 4096,
    googleAuthOptions: {
      keyFilename: '/path/to/service-account.json',
    },
    // Optional host override. location: 'eu' reaches the EU multi-region on its own;
    // set this to route any EU location value, such as europe-west4 above, to that host:
    // apiEndpoint: 'aiplatform.eu.rep.googleapis.com',
  },
  // ...
});
```

Measured on 2026-09-25 against project `re5-n8n-platform`, one `generateContent` call per cell: `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` answers on `aiplatform.eu.rep.googleapis.com` with `location` set to either `eu` or `europe-west4`, and returns 404 on the `europe-west4` regional host. The host routes; the `location` segment does not override it. So a reader who uncomments `apiEndpoint` without touching `location` gets the EU multi-region, which is the point of the field.

`location` selects the regional endpoint, so it decides where the request is processed and which jurisdiction the data stays in - not merely which datacentre is nearest. It is passed straight to `new GoogleGenAI({vertexai: true, location})` and the engine does not validate it: a region that does not serve the model surfaces as a vendor error on the first call, not at construction. The examples here use `europe-west4`.

The `eu` multi-region is a distinct host, `aiplatform.eu.rep.googleapis.com`. `@google/genai` derives it from `location: 'eu'` itself, as it does `us`, and gives any other location its regional host, so `location` alone now reaches it; under `@google-cloud/vertexai` it could not. The optional `apiEndpoint` on `VertexConfig` still overrides the host: it becomes the client's base URL, `https://<apiEndpoint>/`, and when it is absent no base URL is passed, so a deployment that sets only `location` gets the SDK's own choice of host ([validated by: forwards apiEndpoint to the client as its base URL for the eu multi-region](../../src/providers/vertex/vertexProvider.test.ts#L600), [validated by: omits apiEndpoint when unset so single-region deployments are unchanged](../../src/providers/vertex/vertexProvider.test.ts#L615)).

Measured on 2026-10-19 with `@google/genai` 2.26.0 against project `re5-n8n-platform`: a tool-using turn on `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` with `location: 'eu'` reached `aiplatform.eu.rep.googleapis.com` both with `apiEndpoint` unset and set to that host, ran its tool, and sent the call's thought signature back accepted.

- The client is always created in Vertex mode, never against the Gemini API, on Vertex's GA `v1` API, which `@google-cloud/vertexai` called and `@google/genai` would otherwise replace with `v1beta1`, carrying the configured project, location and auth options ([validated by: creates the client in Vertex mode on the v1 API with the configured project, location and auth options](../../src/providers/vertex/vertexProvider.test.ts#L588)).

**Credentials:**

```bash
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/path/to/service-account.json
```

This package reads neither. `createVertexProvider` constructs `new GoogleGenAI({vertexai: true, project, location, googleAuthOptions})` and Google's auth library resolves credentials itself, from `googleAuthOptions`, that variable, or the metadata server. `projectId` is the config field above, not `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT`: setting the variable and passing a different `projectId` gives you the config value with no warning.
</document>
<document source="specs/hal-engine-architecture/spec.md" type="spec" relevance="0.94" tokens="986" truncated="true">
## Tool Execution Loop

At the start of every turn, after the input hooks and before `beforeModelResponse`, the orchestrator reads the `ToolRegistry` once. That read gives it the definitions it sends the provider and the `promptInstructions` of each tool, which `PromptBuilder` assembles alongside the AI's identity, domain context, and response guidelines into the system prompt for that turn.

### What a turn reads from the registry

- A tool registered after the orchestrator was built is sent to the provider and named in the system prompt, under the default tool preamble, from the next turn, so a registry that starts empty may be filled after construction ([validated by: sends weather, registered after the orchestrator was built on an empty registry, as the only tool and names it under the default preamble](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L48)).
- A tool registered between two turns is absent from the first turn's request and present in the second's ([validated by: sends tools undefined on the first turn and weather alone on the second when weather is registered between them](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L65)).
- The read is per turn, not per round: a tool registered while a turn runs, by one of its executors say, reaches no later round of that turn, so the list the model sees does not change while an unanswered `tool_use` block from an earlier round is still in its messages ([validated by: keeps weather, registered by an executor mid-turn, out of round 2 of that turn and sends it from the next turn](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L77)).
- A registry with no tools sends the provider `tools: undefined`, never an empty array, because Bedrock and Vertex read an empty list differently; Bedrock's request then carries no `toolConfig` key ([validated by: calls sendMessage with tools undefined, and Bedrock builds a request with no toolConfig key](../../src/emptyToolList.test.ts#L28)).
- A definition registered as a thunk is read once at the start of each turn for what that turn advertises, so a definition it changes between turns reaches the provider changed ([validated by: invokes a definition thunk once in each of two turns that call no tool, so the second turn sends its changed description](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L99)).
- Each call the turn executes reads the thunk once more, to validate that call's input, so a turn calling the tool once invokes it twice ([validated by: invokes a definition thunk twice in a turn that calls its tool once: at the turn start, then for the call](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L124)).
- A hook sees the same read: `beforeModelResponse` receives a prompt naming a tool registered after `createHalEngine` returned ([validated by: hands beforeModelResponse a prompt naming weather, a tool registered after createHalEngine returned](../../src/config.test.ts#L167)).
- Every tool `description`, `inputSchema` and `promptInstructions` string is sent to the model provider every turn, not once per process, so under GDPR a definition carries no personal data and no internal hostname; the orchestrator logs a tool's name and never its definition ([validated by: writes no tool description, input schema or prompt instructions to the log over a turn that calls the tool](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L142)).

Because the advertised list is re-read every turn, leaving a tool unregistered or unadvertised is not an access control, which matters for the human oversight Article 14 of the EU AI Act asks for. A policy that must keep a capability from being used enforces it at execution, in `beforeToolCall` or in the executor itself. The registry stays process-wide: there is no per-session or per-user tool list, and no `unregister`.

When the AI model decides it needs data (like weather information or a database lookup), it requests a tool call. The orchestrator handles this in a loop:
</document>
</section>
